## **step-by-step instructions** to set up a contact form on your static site that sends emails via **Google Apps Script** using your **Gmail account** — **no backend server needed**, and 100% free.

---

## ✅ What You'll Get

* A simple contact form on your static website.
* When submitted, it sends an email **via your Gmail** using Google Apps Script.
* No need for app passwords or SMTP.

---

## 🧩 STEP 1: Create the Contact Form in Your HTML

Replace or add this form in your HTML file:

```html
<form id="contact-form">
  <input type="text" name="name" placeholder="Your Name" required>
  <input type="email" name="email" placeholder="Your Email" required>
  <textarea name="message" placeholder="Your Message" rows="5" required></textarea>
  <button type="submit">Send</button>
</form>

<p id="status"></p>

<script>
  document.getElementById('contact-form').addEventListener('submit', function (e) {
    e.preventDefault();

    const form = e.target;
    const status = document.getElementById('status');

    fetch('https://script.google.com/macros/s/PASTE_YOUR_DEPLOYED_WEBAPP_URL_HERE/exec', {
      method: 'POST',
      body: new FormData(form)
    })
      .then(response => {
        if (response.ok) {
          status.innerHTML = 'Message sent!';
          form.reset();
        } else {
          status.innerHTML = 'Failed to send message.';
        }
      })
      .catch(error => {
        status.innerHTML = 'Error sending message.';
        console.error(error);
      });
  });
</script>
```

---

## 🧠 STEP 2: Create a New Google Apps Script

1. Go to: [https://script.google.com](https://script.google.com)
2. Click **`New project`**
3. Replace the default code with:

```javascript
function doPost(e) {
  var name = e.parameter.name;
  var email = e.parameter.email;
  var message = e.parameter.message;

  var subject = "New Contact Form Submission";
  var body = "Name: " + name + "\n" +
             "Email: " + email + "\n" +
             "Message:\n" + message;

  MailApp.sendEmail({
    to: "yourgmail@gmail.com",
    subject: subject,
    body: body,
    replyTo: email
  });

  return ContentService.createTextOutput("Success").setMimeType(ContentService.MimeType.TEXT);
}
```

> 🔁 Replace `yourgmail@gmail.com` with your actual Gmail address (can be the same account you're logged into).

---

## 🚀 STEP 3: Deploy the Script as a Web App

1. In the Apps Script editor, click the top-right **Deploy** → **Manage deployments**.
2. Click **`+ New deployment`**
3. Click **`Select type`** → choose **`Web app`**
4. Fill in:

   * **Description**: `Contact form handler`
   * **Execute as**: `Me`
   * **Who has access**: `Anyone`
5. Click **Deploy**
6. You’ll be asked to **authorize** permissions. Approve it.
7. Copy the **Web App URL** you get — looks like:
   `https://script.google.com/macros/s/AKf.../exec`

---

## 🧷 STEP 4: Plug the URL into Your HTML

In your HTML `<script>`, replace this placeholder:

```js
fetch('https://script.google.com/macros/s/PASTE_YOUR_DEPLOYED_WEBAPP_URL_HERE/exec', {
```

with your **real deployed URL**.

---

## 🔐 Important Security Notes

* This is public-facing, so consider adding [Google reCAPTCHA](https://www.google.com/recaptcha/about/) if you get spam.
* You can limit who can access the script by using validation in the `doPost` handler if needed.

---

## ✅ You're Done!

* A form on your static website.
* A no-backend way to send messages via your Gmail using Google Apps Script.
* No need for SMTP, app passwords, or third-party services.

